I'm Johansson Hoover I'm originally from  Holland I've been a clock maker and  repair for a lot of 12 years so the  process to make a wheel is by having a  wheel cast first would you call it blank  you remove the skin of the blank by  filing it off or scraping it off once  you've done that you mark a center hole  a center punch drill a hole in it we use  a dividing plate in order to set out the  correct number of teeth onto the wheel  you then set out the teeth on the wheel  with a device which allows you to mark  or scribe lines on it so if you have a  wheel at 60 teeth you can have you can  mark 60 teeth on to the disk  once you put all the lines on it you  file down beside the line you cut out  the gap in between your teeth  finally you shape the tip of the teeth  and that's done by hand filings and the  last that you do is and file off the  crossings and that's kind of the process  when I cut the wheel and especially  whenever for instance cut the teeth you  get into a flow and like you get into a  rhythm and that kind of like using the  hand tools to shape the teeth or you  know make a crossing is a kind of like  almost therapeutic way of of working you  get kind of into your own world and what  people always forget when you make  something is how much time it takes I  can make a wheel in a day but then  that's only one wheel if you want to  make a whole clock you know a year it's  kind of nothing I always feel very  satisfied when I finish a product or an  item well you have more respect for  things that are being handmade  you
 

